  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nAn area of surface low pressure is expected to move across far \nsouthern Canada Monday night into early Tuesday, bringing a cold \nfront draped to its southwest toward northeast Missouri and west-\ncentral Illinois. This boundary will be of high importance through \nnext week, with its exact location and evolution uncertain for much \nof next week. Conceptually speaking, I do think its more likely than \nnot for the front to ooze southward due to a couple of reasons: 1) \nrepeated rounds of convection along/north of the boundary and 2) \nstrengthening surface high pressure moving into the Upper Midwest. \nConvection tends to help shift the boundary (or effective boundary) \nequatorward via cold pools. The incoming surface high in the Upper \nMidwest also looks quite strong for the middle of September (~1026 \nhPa; >95th percentile of climatology).  This evolution then would \nlead to gradually \"cooler\" temperatures across the region, though \nthe specifics are pretty uncertain especially Wednesday onward. \nThere is more confidence that the front will be mostly to the north \nof the area (with notable exception of northeast Missouri and west-\ncentral Illinois) on Tuesday. Within the warm sector, the expected \nair mass remains pretty extraordinary for this time of year. \nTemperatures at 850 hPa above +20C would be above the 99th \npercentile of climatology, with the strength of the midlevel ridge \nat 500 hPa record-breaking for mid September. In other words, in the \nabsence of cloud cover, near-record breaking high temperatures \nappear possible at both KSTL and KCOU for midweek. Please see the",
